    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Why claim documentation matters</h2>
<p>A property insurance claim is built on evidence. The insurer needs to understand what was damaged, when and how it happened, and what it will cost to fix. Good documentation makes that easier to show and harder to dispute. It can also help if questions come up later about the date of loss, pre-existing damage, wind versus flood, or items missing from the estimate.</p>
<p>The challenge is timing. Evidence fades quickly: water lines dry, wet drywall and flooring are torn out, debris is hauled away, and emergency repairs cover what was there. After a hurricane in Port Arthur, entire neighborhoods may be cleaning up at once. This claim documentation checklist is designed to help you capture the important details while they are still visible.</p>

<h2>Photo and video tips</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Start before cleanup.</strong> Photograph damage before moving anything, then again after emergency mitigation.</li>
<li><strong>Go wide, then close.</strong> Take a wide shot that shows where you are, a medium shot of the damaged area, and close-ups of specific damage.</li>
<li><strong>Show water lines.</strong> If water entered the building, photograph the high-water mark on walls with a tape measure for scale. That helps separate flood damage from wind-driven rain.</li>
<li><strong>Record all sides.</strong> Walk around the entire building and capture each elevation, including fences, outbuildings, vehicles, and HVAC units.</li>
<li><strong>Use video for context.</strong> A slow walkthrough with narration (date, location, what you are seeing) can tie individual photos together.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the originals.</strong> Do not edit or crop the original files; the embedded date and time can be useful.</li>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Do not climb onto a damaged or wet roof, and avoid standing water near electrical equipment. Photograph from the ground or let a professional inspect it.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a step-by-step guide, see <a href="/how-to-document-storm-damage-port-arthur/">how to document storm damage in Port Arthur</a>. Business owners should also gather equipment lists, inventory records, and business income records; our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page explains why.</p>

<h2>Organizing your claim file</h2>
<p>Keep everything in one place, either a physical folder or a cloud folder you can reach from your phone. A simple structure works well:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policies:</strong> each full policy, declarations page, and endorsements, including any TWIA windstorm and NFIP or private flood policy.</li>
<li><strong>Claim basics:</strong> claim numbers, date of loss, adjuster names and contact information for each insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Photos and video:</strong> organized by date and area of the property.</li>
<li><strong>Weather evidence:</strong> storm reports for your date of loss. The <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> can help you find NOAA and National Weather Service reports near your city or ZIP code.</li>
<li><strong>Estimates and invoices:</strong> contractor estimates, the insurer's estimate, mitigation and drying invoices, and receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Contents list:</strong> damaged personal property or business contents with descriptions, ages, and costs where known.</li>
<li><strong>Correspondence:</strong> letters, emails, and your communication log.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Keeping a communication log</h2>
<p>Every conversation about your claim is part of the record. For each call, email, or visit, write down the date and time, who you spoke with, their role, what was discussed, and any next steps or deadlines. Follow up important phone calls with a short email summarizing what was said. A clear log helps you keep track of requests and can be useful if the claim is delayed or disputed.</p>

<h2>Your privacy</h2>
